            simpler: "The bleeding comes from the blocked drain. Heparin stops the clot from growing, the body slowly clears it, the drain reopens, and the pressure that caused the bleeding falls.",
            mechanism: "Heparin does not dissolve the existing clot. It stops the thrombus propagating into more veins and lets the body's own fibrinolysis recanalize the sinus. Because the hemorrhage is a result of venous congestion, relieving the obstruction treats the cause of the bleeding rather than adding to it.",
            compare: "In an arterial stroke, hemorrhagic transformation is a reason to delay antithrombotics. In CVT the logic is reversed: the hemorrhage is a consequence of the venous obstruction, so anticoagulation is still given. IV thrombolysis, an arterial stroke treatment, is not standard for CVT.",
            deeper: "Anticoagulant choice, duration, and the role of thrombophilia testing depend on the cause and are set by guidelines and local protocols. In a person with a transient trigger such as the postpartum period, treatment is usually time limited; with a persistent prothrombotic state it may be longer. Future pregnancies are not forbidden but need a plan with obstetrics and hematology.",
