          body:
            "The aim is to prevent embolic stroke while the vessel heals, which most dissected arteries do over the following months.\n\nAntithrombotic treatment is standard. The CADISS trial randomized patients with recent carotid or vertebral dissection to antiplatelet or anticoagulant drugs for three months. Stroke was rare in both groups and the trial found no clear difference in effectiveness, so the choice is made case by case. Endovascular treatment is reserved for selected patients, such as those with recurrent ischemia despite medical treatment.\n\nAn acute ischemic stroke caused by dissection follows the usual acute stroke pathway. The 2019 AHA/ASA guideline considers IV [[thrombolysis|thrombolysis]] reasonably safe within 4.5 hours in stroke known or suspected to be due to extracranial cervical artery dissection.",
