            compare: "Aneurysmal subarachnoid hemorrhage: usually a single thunderclap, blood in the basal cisterns, an aneurysm on angiography. RCVS: recurrent thunderclaps, a normal scan or only a small convexity bleed, and segmental narrowing that resolves. Migraine builds over minutes to hours; a thunderclap is never typical migraine.",
            deeper: "Avoid vasoconstrictors in suspected RCVS, including triptans for the headaches. Glucocorticoids and other immunosuppressants used for vasculitis do not treat RCVS, so labeling RCVS as vasculitis exposes the patient to treatment without benefit.",
